1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,542
Chapter eleven — the agent loop.

2
00:00:03,602 --> 00:00:10,346
The workbench is not an editor. It is the
thing that tells the editor what to do.

3
00:00:10,406 --> 00:00:17,738
Start from a symbol in the code lens.

4
00:00:17,798 --> 00:00:26,244
Runs on binds this module to a
node on the architecture canvas.

5
00:00:26,304 --> 00:00:36,736
Now the code has a place in the system,
and the node has code behind it.

6
00:00:36,796 --> 00:00:56,526
VS Code opens this file at this line
— the exact one, not the folder.

7
00:00:56,586 --> 00:01:06,519
Annotate attaches an issue
to the symbol itself.

8
00:01:06,579 --> 00:01:18,898
The annotations rail becomes the worklist
— a queue of real, located problems.

9
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:28,902
And the handoff is one click: a fix-ready prompt
with the source, the call graph and your note.

10
00:01:28,962 --> 00:01:40,296
Paste that into Claude Code or VS Code and
the agent starts with the whole picture.

11
00:01:40,356 --> 00:01:43,699
It works the other way too.

12
00:01:43,759 --> 00:01:54,203
Build architecture derives a design from the
repository itself — on-prem, Azure, AWS or GCP.

13
00:01:54,263 --> 00:02:02,704
There is also an MCP server: zen ground, zen
symbol and zen index, straight into Claude Code.

14
00:02:02,764 --> 00:02:08,767
The agent codes. The workbench
keeps the whole system in view.
